2. BSP Kernel and Distros
=========================

The following table summarizes the valid Wind River Linux distros for this BSP.
'Y' in each content cell stands for supported; 'N' stands for not supported:

  +--------------+-------------+------------------+-------------------+--------------+
  | valid/distro |   wrlinux   | wrlinux-graphics | wrlinux-installer | wrlinux-tiny |
  +--------------+-------------+------------------+-------------------+--------------+
  |    valid     |      Y      |        N         |         N         |       N      |
  +--------------+-------------+------------------+-------------------+--------------+

For the supported kernel type for this BSP, check the TARGET_SUPPORTED_KTYPES
by running 'bitbake -e | grep "^TARGET_SUPPORTED_KTYPES"'.

Note: The preempt-rt kernel type can be used with this BSP/Machine.


3. Board Specific Patches
=========================

To get a list of patches applied to the kernel specific to this BSP along with
patch descriptions, use git-whatchanged on the default kernel. For example:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/work-shared/<bsp_name>/kernel-source
	(build-host)$ git whatchanged <kernel_version>/standard/base..<kernel_version>/standard/<bsp_name>


4. Boot Instructions
====================

4.1 Device Trees
----------------
A device tree is a hierarchical data structure of named nodes and properties
used to describe non-discoverable hardware in embedded systems, allowing
the Linux kernel to support various configurations without hardcoding. It
is written in text-based Device Tree Source (.dts) files, compiled into
Device Tree Blob (.dtb) format, and passed to the operating system by the
bootloader at runtime.

The Zynq-7000 boards are required to use the dtb files to boot Linux kernel.
In this BSP, there are two types of device trees: kernel device tree and
system device tree. They satisfy different development needs.

4.1.1 Kernel Device Tree
------------------------
The kernel device tree files describe the hardware components of the Versal
boards, excluding the programming logic (PL). They provide the necessary
information for Linux to understand and interact with the hardware, enabling
the Linux kernel to load appropriate drivers and manage resources.

The kernel device tree source files are located in
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/work-shared/amd-zynq/kernel-source/arch/arm64/boot/dts/xilinx

The following device tree files will be installed to the WIC image along
with the kernel Image:
	zynq-zc702.dtb (used for ZC702)
	zynq-zc706.dtb (used for ZC706)

Note: These device tree files are only used in prebuilt firmware flow for
their less device nodes.

4.1.2 System Device Tree
------------------------
A system device tree comprehensively represents the entire system's
hardware configuration. It encompasses not only the components relevant
to the Linux operating system but also other elements that might be part
of a multi-domain software system. The system device tree provides a
holistic view of the hardware setup, facilitating communication and
coordination across different software domains.

The process of generating a system device tree is described in the section
"7.1.4.3 SDTGen", and it contains the following files:

	- Files that are static for a given device family:
		- soc.dtsi: A SOC specific file containing information about the CPU.
		- board.dtsi: A board file copied from AMD's prebuilt board repository.
		- clk.dtsi: Clock information for the device.
	- Files dynamically generated based on Vivado Design Suite output:
		- pl.dtsi: Contains Programmable Logic(soft IPs) information.
		- system-top.dts: System information about memory, CPU clusters, aliases etc.
		- pcw.dtsi: Information about the configuration of the processing system from
		  the Vivado Design Suite peripheral configuration wizard.

These files will eventually be compiled into one dtb file "system.dtb".
This system.dtb will have more nodes and more properties inside each node
compared to the kernel dtb files mentioned in the section "4.1.1 Kernel
Device Tree".

Note: Unless you are working in the prebuilt firmware flow mentioned in
the section "7.1.2 Prebuilt Firmware", system.dtb is the only dtb file
that you should use to boot Linux.

4.2 Boot Mode
-------------
The Zynq-7000 boards support three boot modes: SD card, QSPI and JTAG.

4.2.1 SD Boot Mode
------------------
First refer to the section "7.1 BOOT.BIN" to build a bootable WIC image and
burn it to SD card at host-pc:

   (build-host)$ sudo dd if=wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-zynq.rootfs.wic of=/dev/sdaX

Plug the SD card into the Zynq-7000 board SD socket and set the DIP
switch setting like:

For ZC706:
   SW11[5]  SW11[4]  SW11[3]  SW11[2]  SW11[1]
    OFF      ON       ON       OFF      OFF

For ZC702:
   SW16[5]  SW16[4]  SW16[3]  SW16[2]  SW16[1]
    OFF      ON       ON       OFF      OFF

Power cycle the board. The board now boots up using the BOOT.BIN in
the SD card.

4.2.2 QSPI Boot Mode
--------------------
First we boot the board with SD Card by following the steps in the section
"4.2.1 SD Boot Mode". At the U-Boot stage, when the message "Hit any key to
stop autoboot:" appears, hit any key, then run the following commands to
burn the image to the QSPI flash:

   (u-boot)> sf probe 0 0 0
   (u-boot)> sf erase 0x0 0x4000000
   (u-boot)> fatls mmc 0:1
   (u-boot)> fatload mmc 0:1 0x70000000 BOOT.bin
   (u-boot)> sf write 0x70000000 0x0 <BOOT.BIN_filesize_in_hex>

Note: The "<BOOT.BIN_filesize_in_hex>" is a hexadecimal value of the size
of BOOT.BIN.

After flashing the images, turn off the power switch on the board, and
change the boot mode pin settings to QSPI boot mode:

For ZC706:
   SW11[5]  SW11[4]  SW11[3]  SW11[2]  SW11[1]
    OFF      ON       OFF      OFF      OFF

For ZC702:
   SW16[5]  SW16[4]  SW16[3]  SW16[2]  SW16[1]
    OFF      ON       OFF      OFF      OFF

Power cycle the board. The board now boots up using the BOOT.BIN in the
QSPI flash.

4.2.3 JTAG Boot Mode
--------------------
The JTAG boot mainly communicates with XSDB and it is out of the scope of
this README. Please refer to the document Zynq-7000 SoC Technical Reference
Manual(UG585).

4.3 Boot Linux From U-Boot
--------------------------
This section provides examples of how to set up U-Boot to boot Linux via
network or SD Card.

4.3.1 Network
-------------
First, you need a host machine with the network servers TFTP and NFS
correctly installed and configured.

Then find the compressed archive rootfs file at:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-zynq/wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-zynq.rootfs.tar.bz2

Extract this file to <nfsroot_path>:
   (build-host)$ sudo tar jxf wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-zynq.rootfs.tar.bz2 -C <nfsroot_path>

Copy the kernel and dtb file to <tftpserver_path>:
   (build-host)$ cp uImage <tftpserver_path>
   (build-host)$ cp system.dtb <tftpserver_path>

Set the U-Boot environment variables and boot:
   (u-boot)> setenv serverip <tftpserver_ip>; setenv ipaddr <board_ip>
   (u-boot)> setenv bootargs console=ttyPS0,115200 earlyprintk root=/dev/nfs rw nfsroot=$serverip:<nfsroot_path> ip=dhcp
   (u-boot)> tftpboot 0x3000000 uImage; tftpboot 0x2a00000 system.dtb; bootm 0x3000000 - 0x2a00000

4.3.2 SD Card
-------------
When the WIC image is built in the meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp or SDT flow,
the U-Boot boot script boot.scr is installed. It contains U-Boot commands
to boot Linux from SD Card automatically. There is no need to reconfigure
U-Boot.

If the WIC image is built in the prebuilt firmware flow, execute the
following commands to set the U-Boot environment variables and boot:
   (u-boot)> setenv bootfile uImage; setenv fdtfile dtb-file-name.dtb; setenv loadaddr 0x3000000; setenv fdtaddr 0x2a00000;
   (u-boot)> setenv bootargs 'console=ttyPS0,115200 earlyprintk root=/dev/mmcblk0p2 rw rootwait'
   (u-boot)> setenv bootcmd 'fatload mmc 0:1 $loadaddr $bootfile; fatload mmc 0:1 $fdtaddr $fdtfile; bootm $loadaddr - $fdtaddr';
   (u-boot)> saveenv; run bootcmd;


5. WIC Notes
============

Users can use the OpenEmbedded Image Creator to create the properly partitioned
image on a SD card. It generates partitioned images from existing OpenEmbedded
build artifacts. Please refer to the following URL for more detailed partition
information about WIC:

https://docs.yoctoproject.org/singleindex.html#creating-partitioned-images-using-wic

This BSP supports disk images for SD card.
After building the project, users will get a WIC image under the directory
tmp/deploy/images/<bsp name>/, such as:

	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-zynq/wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-zynq.rootfs.wic

Note: The WIC image built in WRLinux does not contain the bootloader BOOT.BIN
by default. Please refer to the section "7.1 BOOT.BIN" for the detailed
description about how to create a bootable WIC image.

To burn WIC image to SD card, users only need to execute the below command:

	(build-host)$ sudo dd if=wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-zynq.rootfs.wic of=/dev/your_sd_dev


6. Common Features
==================

6.1 Coresight
-------------

6.1.1 Introduction
------------------

The ARM CoreSight architecture defines four classes of CoreSight components:
access and control, trace source, trace link, and trace sink. Components
of the access and control class provide a user interface to access the
debug infrastructure through memory-mapped locations and JTAG (not involved).

The Zynq 7000 series PS and PL can be debugged together as a complete system
using intrusive and non-intrusive debug techniques. This testing and debugging
capability is based on the ARM CoreSight and consists mostly of ARM-supplied
components, but also includes one Xilinx-supplied component (FTM Programmable
Logic Test and Debug).

6.1.2 Usage and verification
----------------------------

root@QZ706:~#
root@QZ706:~# cd /sys/bus/coresight/devices
root@QZ706:/sys/bus/coresight/devices# ls
etb0  etm0  etm1  funnel0  replicator0  tpiu0
root@QZ706:/sys/bus/coresight/devices#
root@QZ706:/sys/bus/coresight/devices# echo 1 > etb0/enable_sink
root@QZ706:/sys/bus/coresight/devices# cd etm0
root@QZ706:/sys/bus/coresight/devices/etm0# ls
addr_acctype  cntr_rld_event  etmsr	      seq_12_event    subsystem
addr_idx      cntr_rld_val    fifofull_level  seq_13_event    sync_freq
addr_range    cntr_val	      mode	      seq_21_event    timestamp_event
addr_single   ctxid_idx       nr_addr_cmp     seq_23_event    traceid
addr_start    ctxid_mask      nr_cntr	      seq_31_event    trigger_event
addr_stop     ctxid_pid       nr_ctxid_cmp    seq_32_event    uevent
cntr_event    enable_event    power	      seq_curr_state
cntr_idx      enable_source   reset	      status
root@QZ706:/sys/bus/coresight/devices/etm0# echo 1 > enable_source
root@QZ706:/sys/bus/coresight/devices/etm0# cd ../etb0
root@amd-zynq:/sys/bus/coresight/devices/etb0# ls mgmt/
ctl  ffcr  ffsr  rdp  rrp  rwp  sts  trg
root@amd-zynq:/sys/bus/coresight/devices/etb0# cat mgmt/rwp
0x188				<--- changed everytime when cat mgmt/rwp
root@amd-zynq:/sys/bus/coresight/devices/etb0# cat mgmt/rwp
0x1ee				<--- changed everytime when cat mgmt/rwp
root@QZ706:~# cd
root@QZ706:~# dd if=/dev/etb0 of=etb
dd if=/dev/f8801000.etb of=etb
8+0 records in
8+0 records out
4096 bytes (4.1 kB) copied, 0.0466603 s, 87.8 kB/s
root@QZ706:~#

Refer to Documentation/trace/coresight.txt for more information.

6.2 kexec and kdump
-------------------

You need to add feature/kexec support as follow:

	./wrlinux-x/setup.sh --machines amd-zynq --templates feature/kexec,feature/kdump

6.2.1 kexec
-----------

	kexec -l  /boot/zImage --append="`cat /proc/cmdline`"
	kexec -e

6.2.2 kdump
-----------

Add crashkernel=256M@128M to the kernel cmdline
   kexec -p /boot/zImage --append="$your-bootcmd"
   echo c > /proc/sysrq-trigger

7. Xilinx Specific Features
===========================

7.1 BOOT.BIN
------------
This section describes how to generate BOOT.BIN and a WIC image containing
BOOT.BIN.

7.1.1 Introduction
------------------
The AMD SoCs typically have multiple hardware and software binaries used
to boot them to function as designed and expected. These binaries may
include FPGA bitstreams, firmware images, bootloaders, operating systems,
and user-chosen applications. BOOT.BIN is a boot image that stitches these
binary files together.

The following figure shows the components that are included in BOOT.BIN
for Zynq UltraScale+ MPSoC.

	BOOT.BIN
	├── Boot Header
	├── FSBL
	├── Bitstream (optional)
	└── Application Images
	    └── U-Boot (Secondary bootloader)

			Figure 1 BOOT.BIN structure

There are three flows to generate BOOT.BIN and a bootable WIC image, each
flow is suitable for a specific development task:

	- Prebuilt firmware: In this flow, all components of BOOT.BIN are
	  prebuilt. It is for the users who focus on the Linux kernel and
	  userspace development. Refer to the section "7.1.2 Prebuilt Firmware"
	  for the detailed description.

	- Meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp: In this flow, some components (including
	  Bitstream, etc) of BOOT.BIN are prebuilt. They are downloaded by the
	  layer meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp. The other components (including PSM,
	  PLM, ATF, U-Boot) are built in WRLinux with the metadata provided by
	  the layer meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp. It is for the users who have the
	  requirement to reconfigure and build these components. Refer to the
	  section "7.1.3 Meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp" for the detailed description.

	- SDT flow: If users have their own Vivado designs (including PL or PCW),
	  this flow is the only option. In this flow, all hardware information and
	  metadata are extracted from the Xilinx Support Archive (XSA) files.
	  Refer to the section "7.1.4 SDT Flow" for the detailed description.

Note: The examples in this section are for the ZC702 board. For the ZC706
board, just replace the string "zc702" in the command lines with "zc706".

7.1.2 Prebuilt Firmware
-----------------------
The git repository soc-prebuilt-firmware contains the boot asset files
required to rebuild BOOT.BIN for each target board along with a top level
Makefile and simple build targets.

The prebuilt firmware flow is shown below:

     +--------------------------+   +--------+
     |soc-prebuilt-firmware repo|-->|BOOT.BIN|---+
     +--------------------------+   +--------+   |    +---------+
                           +-----------------+   |--->|WIC image|
               +-------+   |uImage           |   |    +---------+
               |WRLinux|-->|zynq-zc702.dtb   |   |
               +-------+   |rootfs.tar.bz2   |---+
                           +-----------------+

			Figure 2 Prebuilt Firmware Flow

Use the following commands to build BOOT.BIN for the ZC702 board:

	(build-host)$ git clone https://github.com/Xilinx/soc-prebuilt-firmware.git
	(build-host)$ cd soc-prebuilt-firmware
	(build-host)$ git checkout xlnx_rel_v2025.2
	(build-host)$ make bootgen_version=xlnx_rel_v2025.2 board=zc702-zynq arch=zynq
	(build-host)$ ls zc702-zynq/BOOT.BIN

The generated BOOT.BIN can be installed to either SD Card or QSPI. You can
refer to the sections "4.2.1 SD Boot Mode" and "4.2.2 QSPI Boot Mode" for
the detailed steps.

Alternatively, you can generate a WIC image with the BOOT.BIN installed by
executing the following steps:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
IMAGE_BOOT_FILES:append = " /< path_to_BOOT.BIN >/BOOT.BIN;BOOT.BIN"
_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

Then, you can refer to the section "5. WIC Notes" to flash this WIC image
to SD card.

Note: The prebuilt firmware flow is only for zc702.

7.1.3 Meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp and meta-xilinx-tools
------------------------------------------------------
7.1.3.1 ZC706 with meta-xilinx-tools
------------------------------------
This section is only for the ZC706 board because ZC706 has NOT be
transitioned from meta-xilinx-tools to meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp.

WRLinux can build BOOT.BIN and create a bootable WIC image for ZC706 by
following the steps as below:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'bootgen'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-tools-xlnx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx-scr'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'xilinx-bootbin'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'device-tree'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'fsbl'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-tools += 'xsct'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-tools += 'external-hdf'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-tools += 'bitstream-extraction'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'fsbl-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'embeddedsw-source'
PREFERRED_PROVIDER_u-boot-tools-native = "u-boot-tools-xlnx-native"
IMAGE_INSTALL:append = " xilinx-bootbin"
WRL_ZYNQ_BOOTBIN_TARGET = "zc706"
BB_NO_NETWORK = '0'
_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake xilinx-bootbin

The BOOT.BIN is located in the following directory:

	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-zynq/boot.bin

The generated BOOT.BIN can be installed to either SD Card or QSPI. You can
refer to the sections "4.2.1 SD Boot Mode" and "4.2.2 QSPI Boot Mode" for
the detailed steps.

Alternatively, you can generate a WIC image with the BOOT.BIN installed by
building wrlinux-image-std instead of xilinx-bootbin:

	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

Then, you can refer to the section "5. WIC Notes" to flash this WIC image

7.1.3.2 ZC702 with Meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp
---------------------------------------------
This section is only for the ZC702 board.
The meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp layer contains the AMD Adaptive SoCs' bsp
features. It provides machine configurations files, multiconfig files,
system device tree files, pl firmwares, kernel configuration fragments etc.

WRLinux uses the metadata of meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp and the recipes
of meta-xilinx to build all the required components, to generate the BIF
file and to call bootgen to create the boot image BOOT.BIN.

The following is the meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp flow diagram:

    +--------------------------+   +------------------------+       +----------+
    |meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp|-->|Yocto machine conf files|---+   |BOOT.BIN  |
    +--------------------------+   +------------------------+   |   |system.dtb|
                     +--------------------------------------+   |-->|boot.scr  |--+
                     |meta-xilinx/meta-xilinx-standalone    |   |   +----------+  |   +---------+
                     |meta-xilinx/meta-xilinx-standalone-sdt|---+                 |-->|WIC image|
                     |meta-xilinx/meta-xilinx-core          |                     |   +---------+
                     |meta-xilinx/meta-microblaze           |                     |
                     +--------------------------------------+   +--------------+  |
                                                    +-------+   |uImage        |  |
                                                    |WRLinux|-->|rootfs.tar.bz2|--+
                                                    +-------+   +--------------+

						Figure 3 Meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp Flow

Use the following commands to build BOOT.BIN for the VCK190 board:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
BB_NO_NETWORK = '0'
DISTRO_FEATURES:append = " xilinx-bootloader"
WRL_ZYNQ_BOOTBIN_TARGET = "zc702"
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'bootgen'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx-scr'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-tools-xlnx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx-uenv'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'xilinx-bootbin'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'fsbl'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'bitstream'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'device-tree'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'python3-anytree'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:virtualization-layer += 'lopper'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:meta-python += 'python3-humanfriendly'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'fsbl-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'embeddedsw-source'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone-sdt += 'sdt-artifacts aiefal aienginev2 audio-formatter avbuf axicdma axicdma-example axidma axidma-example axiethernet axiethernet-example axipcie axipmon axipmon-example axis-switch axivdma axivdma-example bram bram-example can can-example canfd canfd-example canps canps-example cframe cfupmc clk-wiz clk-wiz-example clockps clockps-example common coresightps-dcc csi csi2tx csi2txss csudma csudma-example dcsub ddrcpsu devcfg devcfg-example dfeccf dfeccf-example dfeequ dfeequ-example dfemix dfemix-example dfeofdm dfeofdm-example dfeprach dfeprach-example dfxasm dfxasm-example dmaps dmaps-example dp12 dp12rxss dp12txss dp14 dp14rxss dp14txss dp21 dp21rxss dp21txss dpdma dphy dppsu dsi dsi2rx dsirxss dsitxss dual-splitter emaclite emaclite-example emacps emacps-example emc gpio gpio-example gpiops gpiops-example hdcp1x hdcp22-cipher hdcp22-cipher-dp hdcp22-common hdcp22-mmult hdcp22-rng hdcp22-rx hdcp22-rx-dp hdcp22-tx hdcp22-tx-dp hwicap i2srx i2stx i3c i3cpsx i3cpsx-example iic iic-example iicps iicps-example intc intc-example iomodule ipipsu ipipsu-example llfifo llfifo-example mbox mboxpsu-exampmcdma mcdma-example mipicsiss mipi-rx-phy mipi-tx-phy mmidp mutex mutex-example nandps nandpsu nandpsu-example ospipsv ospipsv-example pciepsu pciepsu-example prc prd qspips qspips-example qspipsu qspipsu-example resetps resetps-example rfdc rfdc-example rtcpsu rtcpsu-example scugic scugic-example scutimer scuwdt sd-fec sdiaud sdi-common sdps sdps-example spdif spi spips spips-example sysmon sysmon-example sysmonpsu sysmonpsu-example sysmonpsv sysmonpsv-example tft tmrctr tmrctr-example tmr-inject tmr-manager trafgen trafgen-example trngpsv trngpsx ttcps ttcps-example uartlite uartlite-example uartns550 uartns550-example uartps uartps-example uartpsv uartpsv-example ufspsxc usb usb-example usbps usbpsu usbpsu-example v-axi4s-remap v-csc v-deinterlacer v-demosaic v-demosaict v-gamma-lfrmbuf-rd v-frmbuf-rd-example v-frmbuf-wr v-frmbuf-wr-exa